    task arbc_v030 "Bump libarbc pin to v0.3.0" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !single_writer
      note "Bump ARBC_GIT_TAG v0.2.0->v0.3.0 (CMakeLists.txt:25); build + all goldens green (v0.3.0 is additive — every 0.2.0 call compiles and behaves unchanged on a single-threaded host). Lands the two library-owned items this editor filed: ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#13 (HostViewport::step() no longer publishes structural writes off the writer thread — it asks Model::on_writer_thread() and reports the owed install as StepOutcome::external_loads_ready) and its companion, the DamageAccumulator flush/drain race (now mutex-guarded for that handoff alone), which together retire A4.1a's writer-priority document lease justification; plus ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#15 (Journal cursor/depth published as atomics, so the UI's per-frame can_undo()/can_redo() at src/dock/dock.cpp:337,343 are any-thread reads). New surface consumed downstream: Document::on_writer_thread(), Document::external_loads_ready(), Document::set_external_load_settler()/external_loads_auto_settled(), HostViewport::Config::external_loads_ready. Prereq for writer_thread. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A4/A4.1/A4.1a. Refinement: tasks/refinements/canvas/arbc_v030.md"
    }
    task history_published_reads "Publish journal entry names for any-thread History-panel reads" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !arbc_v030
      note "arbc::Journal::can_undo()/can_redo()/depth()/cursor() became atomics in v0.3.0 (#15), but entry_at() (arbc/model/journal.hpp:132) did not — it returns a reference into the writer-owned d_entries vector, excluded explicitly by #15. The History panel calls it three times per frame from the UI thread (src/views/views.cpp:187,190,211). Safe today only because the UI thread IS the writer — the exact property editor.canvas.writer_thread removes. Fix: have commands::AppState maintain a published shared_ptr<const std::vector<std::string>> of entry names, refreshed on the writer thread after each commit/navigate and loaded atomically by the panel, so views.cpp reads no journal internals at all. Catch2 coverage for the publish/refresh logic in L1 commands plus a TSan-lane concurrent-read case; existing tests/history_e2e_test.cpp must pass unmodified. Must land BEFORE editor.canvas.writer_thread — otherwise that leaf ships a known TSan race. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/canvas/arbc_v030.md. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A4.1, A4.1a. Refinement: tasks/refinements/canvas/history_published_reads.md"
    }
    task writer_thread "Dedicated writer thread with sync + async closure submission" {
      effort 4d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !arbc_v030, !history_published_reads
      note "libarbc doc 15 § Thread rules (15-memory-model.md:185-202) requires a consumer whose writes originate on two threads to funnel them to ONE dedicated writer thread by posting the work as a task, never to take turns under a mutex — a consumer mutex re-covers accesses, not identity, and the lock-free growth path (relaxed high_water, SlabDirectory::publish, the writer-thread checkpoint seal) is written against one mutator. The editor writes from two threads: the UI thread (every verb, undo/redo, project_open.cpp:107 load_document, save.cpp:126 capture_snapshot) and the render thread (HostViewport's ctor/dtor call the writer-thread-only set_external_load_settler, host_viewport.cpp:115,130, on every add/remove and every resize rebuild). Add an L1 'writer' component (ace::writer::WriterThread over the platform::Threads spawn seam, base+platform only, no libarbc) with one FIFO and two entries — submit_sync (result-carrying verbs; most call sites capture by reference) and submit (coalescing gestures, plus the render thread's deduped external-arrival wake on StepOutcome::external_loads_ready) — an idle hook that PROACTIVELY consumes the work step() deferred (on every queue drain: settle when Document::external_loads_ready() is non-zero, stay armed for a bounded re-poll only while pending_external_loads() is in flight, so an arrival on a fully idle app installs with no submission from anywhere and a document with no external refs costs zero wakeups) — an inline degenerate mode that doubles as the headless-fixture and WASM/A3 fallback, and drain-then-join teardown enclosing the document's lifetime. Retires A4.1a's writer-priority lease (doc_mu/doc_cv/doc_busy/doc_waiting_writers/Impl::Lease, the run() hold, CanvasHost::apply_edit) and moves the edit seam out of L2 render; migrates the per-renderer render-confined KindBridge (canvas_renderer.cpp:40,140-142) to one document-scoped writer-owned bridge, since v0.3.0 runs the settle on the writer and the document holds one settler slot. Tripwire: doc.on_writer_thread() true inside every posted closure, false on the UI and render threads. Promotes tasks/parking-lot.md 'Async submit-queue (off-UI-thread writer)' with its framing corrected — this is a memory-model requirement, not a profiling-gated latency optimization. Scope is the WHOLE migration — a partial one is not a shippable state (a writer thread plus any remaining UI-thread write = two identities, worse than today): the verbs are a rebind of the existing run_edit/apply_edit funnel (shell.cpp:283, project_gateway.cpp:106-343, camera_inspector.cpp:92,118, canvas_view.cpp:403), but project open/create (project_open.cpp:107 load_document, which BINDS the identity, reached from commands/app_state.cpp:93,99 and the shell.cpp:58 bootstrap) and save (save.cpp:128 capture_snapshot + checkpoint) are not on that seam and carry the lifetime restructuring. Only the sync->async tuning of individual gestures is deferrable, under measurement. Supersedes editor.canvas.single_writer's run-on-the-caller seam. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A4/A4.1/A4.1a. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/writer_thread.md"
    }
    task pending_removes_order "A CanvasHost remove pre-empts a still-queued add for the same id" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !writer_thread
      note "CanvasHost::drive_once services removes with a bulk clear (src/render/canvas_host.cpp:319-329) while it consumes pending_resizes/pending_cameras PER-ID — erasing only the requests it actually applied, because step 3's own rule is that a request for an id that is not live YET stays QUEUED. A remove landing after the iteration's pending_adds.swap (:288-291) but before the entry-map lock names exactly such an id: the bulk clear drops it, and the queued add then resurrects the entry the caller removed. DECIDED (parking-lot triage 2026-07-24): a remove PRE-EMPTS a still-queued add — the entry never surfaces. So consume pending_removes per-id like the other two maps, and have a remove for a not-yet-live id cancel that id's queued add (and its queued resize/camera) instead of being dropped; a remove naming an id that is neither live nor queued stays a no-op, not an error, matching add's existing idempotence (:294-296). Catch2 coverage in tests/canvas_host_test.cpp for add-then-remove within one iteration, a remove interleaved between the swap and the lock, and a remove for an unknown id; the existing TSan anchor must pass unmodified. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md 'CanvasHost pending_removes drop window' (D-canvas_host-pending_removes-drop, fixer note during editor.canvas.accent_palette). Design: docs/01-architecture.md A5/A6. Refinement: tasks/refinements/canvas/pending_removes_order.md"
    }
    task arbc_v040 "Bump libarbc pin to v0.4.0" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !writer_thread
      note "Bump ARBC_GIT_TAG v0.3.0->v0.4.0 (CMakeLists.txt:25); the tag landed on ruoso/arbitrarycomposer 2026-07-28, so this leaf is unblocked on the library side. UNLIKE v0.2.0 and v0.3.0 this pin is NOT purely additive — three consumer-visible changes land with it and this leaf OWNS absorbing them. (1) ONE RENDER PATH: the untiled `arbc::render_frame` free function is REMOVED and `render_offline` renders through the same tiled driver the interactive loop uses, separated only by `Exactness`. The editor never called `render_frame` (src/render/render.cpp:30,52 call `render_offline` only), so nothing fails to compile — but a still export composites slightly differently where the two paths disagreed: the retired driver sized its temp to exactly the content, so a minification's outer resampling tap fell on that surface's transparent border and rang the value up. (2) TILE APRON: every tile surface grows 256^2 -> 264^2 device px and paints only its own cell through the new `Backend::composite_windowed`, and a finite `bounds()` is now enforced in the tile's own pixels instead of `compositor.bounded_content_tile_clip`'s device-space round-out clip (retired upstream). The extent boundary therefore antialiases through the composite's own tap rather than clipping to whole device pixels, and the previous up-to-1px of un-attenuated bleed is gone; separately an opaque fill is opaque again across tile boundaries at a fractional composite phase (it was alpha 0.75 every 256 device px on any sub-pixel pan, off-rung scale or rotation — invisible to every landed golden because they all composite at an integral phase). (3) `arbc::Backend` gains `composite_windowed` and `clear_rect` as virtuals a pixel-modelling implementation must supply; the editor implements no `Backend` and has no such double (render.cpp:26,50, canvas_renderer.cpp:183 and tests/binding_test.cpp:9 all use `arbc::CpuBackend`), so this is inert here — it is the one change that would break a future editor-side backend. `SurfaceRef` also gained an optional `origin`; the editor provides no surfaces. EXPECTED TEST CHURN, owned here: re-baseline tests/goldens/export_camera_64x64.png and any byte-exact composite assertion, and JUSTIFY each diff against the v0.4.0 changelog rather than accepting it — a golden that moves for a reason the changelog does not name is a regression, not a re-baseline. Also retarget tests/arbc_pin_test.cpp at v0.4.0: its existing statements stay true (`Document::open` survives unchanged and is still record-only) and it should additionally pin the guarantees the consumer leaves below lean on. THREE PARKED ITEMS CLEAR WITH THE PIN: ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#17 (a deferred-external nested child no longer composites blank under the real WorkerPool) and #18 (a magnified raster leaf renders at the requested scale under BestEffort, so `schedule_follow_up` stops being true forever and the render thread stops burning a core) each get an assertion leaf below; #26 (removed content is reclaimed at the last release once its removal leaves history) needs NO editor change — the insert/delete memory growth is simply gone. ONE CONTRACT CHANGE WITH NO EDITOR WORK: #23 states `Content::bounds()` ANY THREAD and puts a kind with a mutable extent on notice to publish the change atomically; `scene::CameraContent::bounds()` is constant-empty (src/scene/ace/scene/camera.hpp:46-48) and every other kind the editor uses is a library built-in, so `pick_targets`' UI-thread read is now contract-backed and the TSan case in tests/canvas_host_test.cpp stops being a tripwire and becomes an assertion of a stated guarantee. NEW SURFACE CONSUMED DOWNSTREAM, each by its own leaf: `arbc::open_document`/`ReopenedDocument::unreconstructed`/`Document::rebind_content`/`recovered_content_state()`/`set_content_identity_capture` (#19, editor.project.reconstructing_reopen); `Document::create_content_and_attach`/`remove_contents` (#20, editor.cells.one_action_one_entry); `Registry::insert_schema`/`arbc::KindInsertSchema` and org.arbc.solid's new `r,g,b,a,x,y,w,h` grammar (#21/#22, editor.cells.insert_schema); `Journal::history()` (#24, editor.canvas.history_snapshot_adopt); `HostViewport::attach_settler()`/`detach_settler()`/`Config::install_settler` (#25, editor.canvas.settler_attach_split); `render_offline(document, pinned, viewport, backend)` (#27, editor.cameras.export_pinned). Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md triage 2026-07-28. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A1/A5/A6. Refinement: tasks/refinements/canvas/arbc_v040.md"
    }
    task arbc_v041 "Bump libarbc pin to v0.4.1" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !arbc_v040
      note "Bump ARBC_GIT_TAG v0.4.0->v0.4.1 (CMakeLists.txt:25). PURELY ADDITIVE and consumer-inert, unlike v0.4.0: the release is a single upstream memory-safety fix plus one new Content virtual with a correct default, so every v0.4.0 call compiles and behaves unchanged and there is no surface for this leaf to absorb — which is why it carries no refinement of its own (v0.2.0/v0.3.0/v0.4.0 each got one because each absorbed real churn; there is none here). THE FIX: arbc's `NestedContent::inputs()` returned a span into storage owned by its metadata memo and released the memo lock before returning it. The memo is keyed by (snapshot, revision), so that storage is stable only for the ONE binder holding that pin — a second binder attaching a DIFFERENT pin re-keys the memo, rebuilds the edge vector, and frees the buffer under a walker still iterating the span. Two unserialized binds over one document is exactly what this editor does: `sample_cell_color`'s nested arm runs `render_offline` (which binds the whole document) on the UI thread while CanvasHost's render loop binds and walks the same document on its own thread. The compositor's damage router holds such a span across a recursive descent, so it reproduced as a data race AND a heap-use-after-free in `map_damage_up`. Upstream now routes every bind-concurrent walk through the new `Content::visit_inputs`, which holds the kind's own storage lock across the visit. WHY THIS LEAF EXISTS: editor.panels.color_eyedrop_nested's Threading criterion is unsatisfiable on v0.4.0 — its TSan case puts a nested-cell sample on the UI thread concurrent with a live render, which is precisely the racing shape — and ten successive fixers correctly reported no in-scope fix. The bug was NOT introduced by that leaf: the already-shipped `sample_composited_color` (editor.panels.color's plain eyedropper) calls `render_offline` the same way, so any plain eyedropper click on a document containing a nested cell carried the same latent UAF; the new TSan case only made it observable. No editor code change is needed beyond the pin. Verified by the driver's full chain, gcc-tsan included. Upstream: ruoso/arbitrarycomposer v0.4.1 (arbitrarycomposer@352bc6d). Source-of-debt: fixer escalation during editor.panels.color_eyedrop_nested. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A1/A5/A6."
    }
    task nested_real_pool "Assert the deferred-external nested child composites under the real WorkerPool" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !arbc_v040
      note "tests/canvas_host_test.cpp:1622-1628 DOCUMENTS but does not assert the gap: under the real interactive WorkerPool a deferred-external nested child composited BLANK even pre-settled, while the inline degenerate WriterThread composited it byte-exact — and the comment defers the composite assertion to the inline cases, naming `editor.canvas.nested_real_pool` as the follow-up that would own it. That follow-up is this leaf: ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#17 shipped in v0.4.0 (runtime.interactive routes a deferred EXTERNAL nested child's arrival), so the real-pool case can now assert the composite directly. Assert it there, and DELETE the NOTE that says this leaf does not own it — a stale disclaimer pointing at a closed gap is worse than none. The surrounding install-counting assertions (D-writer_thread-10's three idempotent paths) are unaffected and must pass unmodified. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md 'Deferred-external nested child composites blank under real WorkerPool'. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A5. Refinement: tasks/refinements/canvas/nested_real_pool.md"
    }
    task magnified_raster_idle "Assert a magnified raster canvas reaches idle" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !arbc_v040
      note "ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#18 shipped in v0.4.0 (kinds render at the requested scale under BestEffort), so a ~10x magnified 32x32 org.arbc.raster cell no longer reports `schedule_follow_up` true on every step forever — the defect that made the render thread burn a core for as long as the framing held and violated 02-architecture#idle-viewport-issues-no-frames. Assert the fix: a case that frames a raster cell with Shift+F (editor.canvas.nav_aids) and REQUIREs the canvas reaches quiescence — `frames_issued` stops advancing with an empty in-flight tile queue — at magnification, mirroring the 1x raster and nested-solid fixtures in the same file that were already quiet. KEEP tests/canvas_nav_e2e_test.cpp:139's wall-clock-bounded `settle()` helper exactly as it is: it is lane protection against ANY non-idling canvas, not a workaround for this one bug, and unbounded it tripped the ImGui Test Engine's 60 s ConfigWatchdogKillTest and turned a 16 s ace_shell_test into a 566 s failure. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md 'A magnified raster cell never lets the canvas go idle'. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A5/A6. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/magnified_raster_idle.md"
    }
    task render_loop_liveness_wake "Fix render-loop lost-wakeup when nested raster tiles resolve async (blank-first-frame liveness)" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !blank_first_frame
      note "Rare lost-wakeup in the render loop's blank-first-frame guard: blank_first_frame withholds the published sequence on a blank first frame (canvas_host.cpp:444-448), and loop liveness through the blank phase rests solely on step() witnessing in-flight work via !pending().tiles.empty() (canvas_renderer.cpp:290). If that witness is momentarily false while a nested raster is still resolving async, the loop goes cold at sequence 0 and published_sequence never advances (30s stall in D-writer_thread-10's canvas_host_test.cpp anchor at line 1286). Fix: add an async worker→render-loop completion wake so the loop re-enters when tile work completes without spinning, OR add a resolving-vs-empty liveness distinction in the blank-phase guard so it stays armed while raster work is pending but not yet tiled. A naive keep-spinning-while-!content_published busy-loops genuinely-empty documents — canvas_host.cpp:444-446 guards against that — so the fix must distinguish resolving from empty. Surfaced as a rare flake during editor.cells.group_transform ASan/TSan anchor (passed 26/26 prior runs; confirmed not a group_transform regression). Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/group_transform.md (fixer-attempt-2, 2026-07-29). Design: docs/01-architecture.md A5. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.canvas/render_loop_liveness_wake.md"
    }
    task history_snapshot_adopt "Read the library's published history snapshot; retire the host-side mirror" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !arbc_v040
      note "ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#24 shipped `Journal::history()` in v0.4.0: an any-thread pinned immutable snapshot (the `Model::current()` shape) carrying exactly the projection a panel draws — entry name plus per-entry byte_cost — with unchanged rows shared by pointer, so a commit copies N pointers rather than N strings and an undo republishes without allocating a row. That is the library-side answer to the ask editor.canvas.history_published_reads answered host-side under A18. Retire `commands::HistoryPublisher` (src/commands/ace/commands/app_state.hpp:118-123,202-207, the `publish_history` free function at :293, and every writer-thread refresh call site) and have the History panel read `journal().history()` directly. TWO PROPERTIES MUST NOT REGRESS: src/views/views.cpp still reads no journal internals other than a published snapshot (the A18 property — `entry_at()` stays writer-thread-only upstream, deliberately, so a direct call there is still a race); and the multi-step click-to-jump result seam (app_state.hpp:261-281, D-history_published_reads-4) stays in L1 commands — it is a VERB, not a read, and is untouched by this. tests/history_e2e_test.cpp must pass unmodified; tests/history_publish_test.cpp is retargeted at the library snapshot or retires with the publisher it covers. Per-entry byte cost becomes available to the panel for the first time — DISPLAYING it is editor.panels.history's call, not this leaf's. DECIDED (parking-lot triage 2026-07-28): retire the mirror rather than keep two publishers of the same fact. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md 'arbc::Journal entry_at()/byte_cost() — upstream any-thread publication'. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A18, A4.1. Refinement: tasks/refinements/canvas/history_snapshot_adopt.md"
    }
    task settler_attach_split "Let the render thread own HostViewport lifetime via attach/detach_settler" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      depends !arbc_v040, !pending_removes_order, !arbc_router_attach_split
      note "ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#25 shipped `HostViewport::attach_settler()`/`detach_settler()` plus `Config::install_settler = false` in v0.4.0 — precisely the seam D-writer_thread-8 asked for. Today the render thread posts the WHOLE HostViewport construction and destruction to the writer thread through submit_sync (src/render/canvas_renderer.cpp:73,150; src/render/canvas_host.cpp:116) for one line inside the ctor: `Document::set_external_load_settler` is writer-thread-only. That costs a synchronous round trip per canvas add, per remove and per resize rebuild, and gives up scoped ownership of the viewport. Construct with `install_settler = false` on the render thread, post ONLY the `attach_settler()`/`detach_settler()` pair, and the render thread owns the viewport lifetime again. Upstream leaves the install counting and the N-viewports-per-document rule unchanged — only WHEN they happen moves — so the proof obligation is the existing TSan anchors in tests/canvas_host_test.cpp passing unmodified, plus the tripwire that `doc.on_writer_thread()` holds inside the attach/detach closures and NOWHERE else (a construction that quietly stayed posted would pass every test while delivering none of the benefit). ORDERING: depends on editor.canvas.pending_removes_order deliberately, not just on the pin. Both leaves rewrite `CanvasHost` — pending_removes_order changes how drive_once CONSUMES the pending maps (src/render/canvas_host.cpp:288-329), this leaf changes how the viewport entries those maps name are CONSTRUCTED and destroyed. Landing the 0.5d one first means it is written against the host as it stands today rather than rebased onto a restructured lifetime, and it keeps the add/remove pre-emption rule settled before the construction path moves underneath it. The reverse order costs a rebase of the smaller leaf for no gain. RE-DEFER 2026-07-28 (NOW RESOLVED): arbc#25 split only the settler slot; DamageRouter::register_sink remained welded to the ctor, so render-thread construction raced the router. The successor was filed as arbc#28 at the 2026-08-07 triage and SHIPPED IN v0.5.0 — `Config::install_damage_sink` plus `attach_damage_sink()`/`detach_damage_sink()` defer BOTH installs, so every writer-thread-only mutation in a viewport lifetime is now reachable from one postable closure. This leaf is unblocked once arbc_router_attach_split lands; the three-times-re-deferred placeholder that stood in its way is retired into editor.canvas.arbc_v050. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md 'HostViewport settler attach/detach split'; tasks/refinements/editor/writer_thread.md D-writer_thread-8. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A4.1/A5. Refinement: tasks/refinements/canvas/settler_attach_split.md"
    }
    task grid "Composition grid: display toggle + spacing setting + snap-to-grid wiring" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.cells.gizmo
      note "The composition grid docs/00-design.md:260 names: a display toggle + spacing setting (local UI state), and feeding the grid lines into the gizmo's already-general snap_placement engine (interact/pick.hpp) so a placement snaps to grid. Deferred from editor.cells.gizmo because snap-to-an-invisible-grid with no spacing control is a non-affordance; the snap engine was built to consume grid lines the moment a grid exists. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/gizmo.md. Design: docs/00-design.md D7/D8 §6. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/grid.md"
    }
    task arbc_v070 "Bump libarbc pin v0.4.1 -> v0.7.0 (absorbing v0.5.0 and v0.6.0 unconsumed)" {
      effort 2.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !arbc_v041
      note "Bump ARBC_GIT_TAG v0.4.1->v0.7.0 (CMakeLists.txt:25), SKIPPING v0.5.0 AND v0.6.0 as pins. v0.7.0 (2026-08-09) is a ONE-ISSUE, PURELY ADDITIVE release closing arbc#39 — `KindMetadata::modality`, a `KindModality` FLAG SET over Visual/Audio defaulting to Both, plus `intersects()` and a widened `Registry::insert_offer(id, presentable = KindModality::Both)`. Its only consumer note is that a host compiled against v0.6.0 RECOMPILES because the `insert_offer` signature changed, and needs NO source edit — and this editor does not call `insert_offer` at all yet (editor.cells.insert_offer is the leaf that will), so v0.7.0 adds NOTHING to this leaf's absorption work beyond retargeting the tag and the pin test. The built-ins now declare truthfully — tone Audio, solid/raster/image/imageseq Visual, nested and the operator kinds Both — and upstream pins each declaration against what a constructed instance actually exposes, so the declarations cannot drift. THE ORIGINAL v0.6.0 SKIP RATIONALE, UNCHANGED AND NOW APPLYING TO TWO INTERMEDIATE PINS: v0.5.0 shipped 2026-08-08 and v0.6.0 the same week, and NOTHING here ever consumed v0.5.0 (this leaf was still READY-unstarted when v0.6.0 landed), so bumping through it would mean absorbing two sets of churn in two passes for no gain. Absorb both at once. THE v0.6.0 HALF FIRST, BECAUSE IT IS THE ONLY BREAKING ONE. ruoso/arbitrarycomposer v0.6.0 closes #35-#38, all four filed by the 2026-08-08 triage. ITS CHANGELOG SAYS THE ONE BREAKING CHANGE IS `only for a third-party backend` — THAT IS ABOUT IMPLEMENTING `Backend`, AND IT UNDERSTATES THE IMPACT HERE. This editor implements no Backend (correct, it uses `arbc::CpuBackend`) but it CALLS `composite` directly in three places, and `Backend::composite`/`composite_clipped`/`composite_windowed` each gained a REQUIRED `BlendMode blend` parameter immediately after `opacity` — required rather than defaulted on purpose, because a default argument on a virtual binds statically and would let backend and caller disagree. So THESE FOUR SITES FAIL TO COMPILE and this leaf owns them: src/render/dim_scrim.cpp:93, src/render/render.cpp:75, src/render/canvas_renderer.cpp:188, and the `static_assert` on `&arbc::Backend::composite_windowed` at tests/arbc_pin_test.cpp:349 (a member-function-pointer type that moved). Every one passes `1.0` opacity and wants plain source-over, so each takes `arbc::BlendMode::Normal`, which the release states is premultiplied source-over EXACTLY with its own kernel arm — so NO GOLDEN MAY MOVE, and one that does is a regression to investigate, not a re-baseline. ALSO FROM v0.6.0, all additive: `LayerRecord` carries a `BlendMode` set via `Model::Transaction::set_blend` and read via `LayerRecord::blend()`, persisted BY NAME and omitted when normal — the editor exposes no blend control (D10 forbids it for v1) so it writes none, but a document authored elsewhere may carry one and MUST round-trip: an unknown name is preserved verbatim and rendered source-over, so the save path must not drop the field. `render_offline` gained a defaulted trailing `OfflineRenderReport*` out-param (#35) consumed by editor.cameras.export_unresolved_report. `KindMetadata` gained a defaulted trailing `insertability` field and `Registry::insert_offer(id)` arrived (#37), consumed by editor.cells.insert_offer — NOTE that until that leaf lands the editor KEEPS offering `org.arbc.fade` and `org.arbc.crossfade`, which now declare themselves Internal, because the editor enumerates `registry.ids()` rather than `insert_offer`. #38 was answered as documentation with NO behaviour change; see editor.cameras.model for why it is benign here. THEN THE v0.5.0 HALF, unchanged from the 2026-08-08 triage and all still to be absorbed here. ruoso/arbitrarycomposer tagged v0.5.0 on 2026-08-08 closing SEVEN issues (#28-#34) — every one filed by this editor at the 2026-08-07 parking-lot triage, and every one a parking-lot item that closes with this pin. REPLACES the `arbc_router_split_pin` placeholder: that leaf existed only to name `the release that splits the DamageRouter`, v0.5.0 IS that release, and it carries six other things the placeholder never scoped, so the honest shape is a normal pin-bump leaf in the arbc_v020/v030/v040/v041 series rather than a 0.5d placeholder pretending the rest is free. ADDITIVE except one enumerator, so nothing fails to compile — but four things need absorbing and one needs only a re-read. (1) `KindInsertField::Type` GAINS AN `ObjectId` ENUMERATOR. `scene::map_field_type` (src/scene/cell.cpp:32-42) switches over that enum with NO default, and the build sets no -Werror/-Wswitch (CMakePresets.json carries no warning flags), so this COMPILES and silently falls through to `InsertFieldType::Text`. That is not wrong — the dock POD drops the type entirely and the L3 modal renders every field as free text (cell.hpp:44-46), and upstream states an undistinguishing host `collects the field exactly as an Integer and stays correct` — but it throws away the one bit the new type carries. Add the `ObjectId` case and a matching editor-side enumerator so the hint survives; a composition picker over it is a LATER call, not this leaf. (2) `org.arbc.nested` NOW ADVERTISES AN INSERT SCHEMA (#33) — one labelled `child` field of the new type, min 1, no default. The editor needs NO code for this beyond (1): the field appears through the no-allowlist enumeration automatically (`insert_schemas` emits one entry per `registry.ids()` entry, cell.hpp:36-42). VERIFY it renders labelled rather than as the raw-config box, which is the whole point of the parked item it closes. (3) `RasterContent::bounds()` IS NOW DERIVED from the live tile table rather than cached at construction, so extent and pixels cannot disagree after a resample. Re-baseline nothing blindly: a golden that MOVES here needs justifying against the changelog, and none is expected for documents that never resample. (4) `gc_project_directory` NOW MARKS `params.source` URIs AND SWEEPS `assets/images/` (#30) — its two-arg signature is UNCHANGED (asset_gc.hpp:179-180), so src/project/gc.cpp:81 compiles untouched and gains the behaviour for free. Two consequences to re-read rather than assume: gc.cpp:65 reasons about an empty referenced set, which is no longer the same set; and a MALFORMED `params.source` now FAILS the mark walk where the image codec's load path still treats it leniently as absent, so a project with a bad source URI now yields a GcError where it previously swept — confirm the editor's report/error mapping still reads sensibly to a user. (5) INERT: `AssetReaper` gained three virtuals but they are DEFAULTED to an empty store and the editor implements no reaper. ALSO RETARGET tests/arbc_pin_test.cpp at v0.5.0 with compile-time witnesses for the new surface the consumer leaves below lean on: `HostViewport::attach_damage_sink`/`detach_damage_sink`/`Config::install_damage_sink`, `Document::update_content_config`/`undo`/`redo`/`reconcile_content_bindings`/`set_content_reconstruct`, `codec_content_reconstruct`, `install_external_composition`, `Content::resampleable`/`Resampleable`, `GcRoots`/`collect_referenced_assets`. NEW SURFACE CONSUMED DOWNSTREAM, each by its own leaf: the damage-sink split (#28, editor.canvas.arbc_router_attach_split); the nested worker-dispatch fix (#29, editor.canvas.nested_real_pool_restore); the GC image mark/sweep (#30, editor.project.gc_owned_images); `Resampleable` (#31, editor.cells.resample_apply); `install_external_composition` (#32, editor.import.nested_live_resolve); `update_content_config` + the undo reconcile (#34, editor.project.undo_reconcile then editor.import.consolidate). Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md triage 2026-08-08. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A1/A5/A6. Refinement: tasks/refinements/canvas/arbc_v070.md"
    }
    task arbc_router_attach_split "Construct HostViewport on the render thread: defer BOTH writer-thread installs" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      depends !arbc_v070
      note "UNBLOCKED by v0.5.0 (#28 closed 2026-08-08); consume the new API: construct HostViewport on the render thread with both the settler slot and the DamageRouter registrant deferred (install_settler=false + the router-split flag), post the now-complete attach pair via `attach_settler()`/`detach_settler()` AND the new `attach_damage_sink()`/`detach_damage_sink()`, both idempotent. THE ACTUAL SHIPPED SHAPE (verified at v0.5.0, host_viewport.hpp:128-133): the flag is `Config::install_damage_sink`, a SEPARATE flag beside `install_settler` rather than the single fused one this note guessed at, and unlike `install_settler` it is honoured on BOTH constructors — the bare-`Model&` unit-test ctor installs a damage sink too. The default stays TRUE (fused), which is right for a single-threaded host, so this leaf must set BOTH flags false explicitly. This satisfies settler_attach_split's Constraint 3 — the single posted closure carries every writer-thread-only mutation — so settler_attach_split unblocks. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/canvas/settler_attach_split.md (re-defer 2026-07-28). Design: docs/01-architecture.md A4.1/A5."
    }
    task nested_real_pool_restore "Retire the inline-pool workaround: nested cells render on the real interactive pool" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      depends !arbc_v070
      note "ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#29 shipped in v0.5.0: a nesting content is never handed to a worker. The defect was NOT the one this editor first described — it is not merely an unsynchronized `d_doc` read. `org.arbc.nested` projects its input edges from its child composition membership, so a child that is EMPTY, not yet loaded, or unresolvable left `inputs()` empty, and the leaf-only dispatch rule (which classifies on inputs alone) handed the render to the pool; that render then dereferenced the frame borrowed DocRoot pin which the per-frame OperatorBindingScope nulls and releases at frame end, while a worker task outlives the frame BY DESIGN. Upstream now classifies a nesting content on its structural `composition_ref()` edge beside its inputs, and `NestedContent::render`/`render_audio` snapshot their borrowed services once under the same lock attach/detach take and answer an unbound render as a VALUE rather than dereferencing. THIS LEAF REMOVES THE WORKAROUND, which is spread wider than one file: every live-canvas e2e that renders `org.arbc.nested` was scaffolded with the inline `arbc::WorkerPoolConfig{}` pool plus the long-deadline test-seam CanvasView ctor instead of `default_interactive_pool_config()` — the overview e2e explicitly (it SIGSEGVed across five fix attempts before the workaround landed), and isolation_scope, cells_scoped_edit and the overview_gizmo e2e latently. Flip each back to the real interactive pool and DELETE the memo comments that tell the next author to use the inline one, since a stale workaround note outlives the bug it documents and this whole leaf exists because that already happened once. KEEP the inline pool anywhere it is load-bearing for DETERMINISM rather than for this bug — a byte-exact golden that wants single-threaded dispatch is not a workaround — and say which is which in the commit. The gcc-tsan lane is the proof obligation: it must be green with the real pool, not merely not-crashing. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md `arbc nested-render worker-detach race` (filed as arbc#29 2026-08-07, closed in v0.5.0); the `arbc-nested-render-worker-detach-race` memo. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A5. "
    }
