    }
    task mint_from_focused_canvas "Promote the focused canvas's framing, not canvas#1's" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !new_shot_from_view
      note "CanvasView::primary_framing() (src/app/canvas_view.cpp:523-534) always returns the lowest-id sized presenter, so with two canvases open 'New Shot From View' promotes canvas#1 even when the user works in canvas#2. Add focused-canvas view id to CanvasView (set from ImGui::IsWindowFocused at draw time, fallback to lowest-id), expose focused_framing(), bind the gateway's framing provider to it, move insert_cell's provisional placement to the same source, and add a multi_canvas e2e. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/cameras/new_shot_from_view.md. Design: docs/00-design.md D23, D18. Refinement: tasks/refinements/cameras/mint_from_focused_canvas.md"
    }
    task export "Export via cameras (render_offline); PNG; batch" {
      effort 3d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !model
      note "A camera IS the export spec: pick camera(s) -> library render_offline each at its resolution -> file(s). PNG (working->sRGB8); batch (one file per camera) + contact sheet fall out; transparent/filled bg, N-times scale multiplier; async with progress. Design: D14. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.cameras/export.md. Decided (pre-exec 2026-07-19): default background TRANSPARENT (preserves alpha; filled bg is a deliberate opt-in); N× multiplier default 1×; on filename collision OVERWRITE in place (batch export is idempotent, mirroring Save's re-dump — no auto-suffix, no prompt); contact sheet = uniform grid, each camera rendered then scaled to fit a common tile box preserving aspect, ceil(sqrt(N)) columns, thin gutters, a small per-tile camera-name caption, on the chosen background. NOTE: render_offline binds nested-composition operators as of libarbc v0.2.0 (ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#6, fixed) — nested children export correctly with the v0.2.0 pin (editor.canvas.arbc_v020)."
    }
    task export_unresolved_report "Report the blank regions an export could not resolve" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      depends editor.canvas.arbc_v070, editor.cameras.export_pinned
      note "ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#35 shipped in v0.6.0. Upstream did NOT make `render_offline` settle — deliberately, since a byte-exact reference path must render exactly the version it was handed and must not publish a revision underneath its caller. It fixed THE SILENCE instead, which is the shape this editor asked for: `render_offline` takes an optional trailing `OfflineRenderReport*` whose `unresolved_contents` counts the contents in the frame it drew that name an external reference which is not there, and `SequenceRenderer::unresolved_contents()` answers the same for a whole export (one number, since the pin is taken once). `count_unresolved_contents` (arbc/runtime/unresolved_contents.hpp) is the walk itself, public for asking BEFORE rendering. Thread the report through `render::render_document_srgb8_pinned` and its `_over_pinned` sibling into the export batch, and surface a non-zero count to the user — the natural home is the existing export-completion notice beside `document_changed_during_export`, since both answer `is this PNG what you meant?`. THREE PROPERTIES UPSTREAM STATES THAT THE UI COPY MUST NOT CONTRADICT: the count is against the RENDERED PIN, never `Document::pending_external_loads()` (an arrival installs on a NEW revision, so the document's own count can fall to zero while the pin still draws the placeholder); PENDING AND UNAVAILABLE COUNT ALIKE, because they differ by whether the source answered, which is the loader's distinction and not the exporter's — so the message must not promise `still loading, try again`; and the count is of blank REGIONS, so two contents sharing one unresolved URI count TWO. WHY THIS IS WORTH A LEAF THOUGH IT IS LATENT: the editor's shipped `FilesystemAssetSource` answers inline, so a production export today resolves everything and the count is always zero — but the failure it guards is a SILENT transparent hole in a delivered PNG, and wiring the report now costs one out-param while the alternative is discovering it from a user. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md `render_offline does not settle deferred external loads` (arbc#35). Design: docs/00-design.md D14; docs/01-architecture.md A20."
    }
    task contact_sheet "Contact sheet: all cameras tiled into one PNG" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !export
      note "Tile the same ExportPlan into one additional PNG: uniform grid, ceil(sqrt(N)) columns, each tile scaled to fit a common tile box preserving aspect (linear-working-space resample via arbc/media/image_resampler.hpp in L2 render, not an sRGB8 box filter), thin gutters, a small per-tile camera-name caption via an embedded fixed-width bitmap glyph table (ImGui font is L3-only, unavailable to a headless job). Chosen background matches the Export panel bg option. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor.cameras/export.md. Design: docs/00-design.md D14, :362; docs/01-architecture.md A20. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/contact_sheet.md"
    }
    task caption_latin1 "Contact-sheet captions: Latin-1 camera names" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !contact_sheet
      note "Decode the camera name from UTF-8 to code points inside commands, extend the embedded glyph table with the printable Latin-1 range (U+00A0–U+00FF), drive text_width/fit_text/draw_text off code points instead of bytes, and keep the fallback box for anything outside the table. Motivation: camera names are free text with no charset constraint (editor.cameras.rename_stable_id), and D3/D23's arbitrariness principle argues against silently boxing a name the user typed — 'Café' rendering as 'Caf□' is a visible defect. Tests: table-driven Catch2 cases over accented names and malformed UTF-8, plus one golden with an accented caption. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/contact_sheet.md. Design: docs/00-design.md D23. Refinement: tasks/refinements/cameras/caption_latin1.md"
    }
    task export_destination_reseed "Re-seed Export destination when service is pointed at a new project" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !contact_sheet
      note "The Export panel seeds its destination path (exports/ under the project directory) once when the ExportService is first created; on project reopen the panel retains the previous project's destination because the panel keyed its reset on the service pointer address, not a stable identity. Re-seed the destination whenever the service instance id changes, so the path always reflects the live project. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/contact_sheet.md (fixer follow-up). Design: docs/01-architecture.md A20. Refinement: tasks/refinements/cameras/export_destination_reseed.md"
    }
    task export_pinned "Pin the document once per batch export so every item renders one state" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.canvas.arbc_v040, !export_destination_reseed
      note "ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#27 shipped `render_offline(document, pinned, viewport, backend)` in v0.4.0 (arbc/runtime/offline.hpp:42) beside the pin-per-call overload. The pin-per-call shape is why an N-frame batch export pinned N times, so an edit landing mid-batch could make item 3 reflect a document state item 1 did not. D-export-8 chose to RECORD and REPORT that incoherence rather than block it (`ExportReport::document_changed_during_export`, src/commands/ace/commands/export.hpp:193, computed at export.cpp:377 as the end revision differing from the start revision, surfaced at src/views/views.cpp:463) because the host-side alternatives were untenable: blocking the writer for a whole batch contradicts D14's async promise, and snapshotting meant a full parse-and-rebuild per export. Take the pin ONCE for the batch and pass it to every item. A `DocStatePtr` retains its version, so there is no 'that revision is gone' error case a revision number would have needed. THE REPORTING FIELD THEN CHANGES MEANING and must not be left as-is: with one pin, the batch IS coherent, so `document_changed_during_export` stops being a coherence warning about the OUTPUT and becomes at most an informational note that the document moved on while the export ran. Either retire it with its views.cpp notice, or keep it and reword the notice to say what is now true — a stale warning telling the user their coherent export might be incoherent is worse than none. Cancellation stays honest at ITEM granularity (Constraint 10, export.cpp:250) — unchanged; a pin held across a cancelled batch just releases earlier. tests/export_test.cpp's revision cases are where the new invariant is pinned: an edit committed mid-batch must leave every item byte-identical to a batch with no concurrent edit. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md 'render_offline version-addressed offline render'. Design: docs/00-design.md D14, docs/01-architecture.md A20. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.cameras/export_pinned.md"
    }
    task truetype_captions "Bundled TrueType face for offline text; retire the bitmap glyph table" {
      effort 2.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !caption_latin1, editor.canvas.arbc_v040
      note "DECIDED (parking-lot triage 2026-07-28): ship a real bundled font and retire the embedded bitmap glyph table. editor.cameras.contact_sheet chose an embedded 5x7 constexpr ASCII table (D-sheet-4) as the defensible interim and editor.cameras.caption_latin1 extended it to the Latin-1 printable range; this leaf replaces both. THE FACE AND ITS LICENCE (decided 2026-07-28, human sign-off): bundle INTER REGULAR under the SIL OPEN FONT LICENCE 1.1, filling the `assets/ icons, fonts (bundled)` slot docs/01-architecture.md:247 has reserved and nothing has ever filled. OFL 1.1 explicitly permits bundling and redistribution inside software, commercially included, and Inter carries NO RESERVED FONT NAME — which is the load-bearing property here, because OFL's RFN clause forces a rename on any MODIFICATION and subsetting the face to the Latin-1 range this leaf needs IS a modification. Inter is additionally designed for small-size legibility and to work UNHINTED, which is exactly the regime stb_truetype imposes (it ignores hinting entirely, so a face that leans on strong hinting — DejaVu Sans, say — surrenders its advantage here). TRAP: Inter ships as a VARIABLE font by default and stb_truetype does not support variable axes. Vendor the STATIC `Inter-Regular.ttf` with TrueType `glyf` outlines, which is stb's best-tested path — never `InterVariable.ttf`, never a CFF/OTF build. ALTERNATIVE, if a more conservative face is ever wanted: DejaVu Sans (Bitstream Vera plus Arev licence, equally permissive, ubiquitous on the Linux v1 target) — that is a one-line change to this decision, not a redesign. THIS LEAF ESTABLISHES THE REPO'S FIRST OUT-OF-BAND LICENCE RECORD: third-party licences are recorded NOWHERE today. `third_party/stb/stb_image_write.h` carries its dual MIT/public-domain text INLINE in the header, which works for source and cannot work for a binary `.ttf`, and there is no root LICENSE and no third_party/LICENSE. So ship `OFL.txt` beside the font binary and state the vendored set (the font plus stb_truetype) somewhere editor.packaging.package will carry INTO the installed artifact. A bundled font whose licence text does not travel with it is the actual legal exposure, and it is created at packaging time, not at vendoring time. EMBED, DO NOT LOAD AT RUNTIME: the 5x7 table's quiet virtue was that it was constexpr — no file I/O, no runtime asset path, nothing for a headless commands-level job to fail to find and no FILE* route around platform::FileSystem. Preserve that by turning the `.ttf` into a generated constexpr byte array at BUILD time (a CMake step), so the face is linked in exactly as the table was. A runtime font path would make caption rendering depend on installed-artifact layout, which is a new failure mode for the offline job and a regression against D-sheet-4's reasoning. THE DEPENDENCY IS THE EASY PART: `third_party/stb/stb_truetype.h` goes beside the already-vendored `stb_image_write.h` under the identical containment idiom — instantiated by exactly ONE TU, a PRIVATE include dir on ace_commands (CMakeLists.txt:192-198), and a new scripts/check_levels.py EXTERNAL_ALLOWED entry confining it to {'commands'} exactly as `stb_write` is confined today. THE REAL BLOCKER IS THE PIPELINE, not the header: D-sheet-2's copy-only invariant. stb_truetype rasterizes an 8-bit COVERAGE bitmap, so a glyph edge is an ALPHA BLEND and not a copy of an opaque texel. src/commands/glyphs.cpp's text_width/fit_text/draw_text and the contact-sheet tile composite (src/commands/contact_sheet.cpp) must composite coverage over the chosen background in the LINEAR working space, never as an sRGB8 blend. AMEND D-sheet-2 to scope the copy-only invariant to the tile IMAGE path and admit compositing for text — that scoping is the whole reason the interim table existed. KEEP the white-plus-black-shadow legibility pair: antialiasing makes an arbitrary background easier to read against, not unnecessary. RETIRE the 5x7 table, its Latin-1 extension and its fallback box — a real face covers Latin-1 natively and reports its own .notdef, so the missing-glyph box becomes the font's business — but keep a case pinning that an unmapped code point still renders SOMETHING rather than nothing. TESTS: the contact-sheet goldens re-baseline because caption shapes change; this leaf depends on editor.canvas.arbc_v040 so the tile-apron re-baseline settles FIRST and the captions are re-baselined once instead of twice. caption_latin1's accented-name and malformed-UTF-8 cases are CHARSET cases, not glyph-table cases, and must survive the swap unmodified. Headless stays headless: ImGui's font atlas is L3-only and unreachable from a commands-level job, which is why the table existed; stb_truetype has the same property, so the offline path keeps working. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md 'Bundled font for offline text rendering' (DECIDED, item closed 2026-07-28). Design: docs/00-design.md D14, docs/01-architecture.md A20, :247. Refinement: tasks/refinements/cameras/truetype_captions.md"
    }
  }

  # ---- Cells & manipulation (D3/D7/D8) ----
  task cells "Cells & manipulation" {
    task model "Insert cell (image/raster/nested/solid) via Registry" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.canvas.view
      note "Insert a cell of any registered kind (org.arbc.image/raster/nested/solid) off the library Registry::ids(); place it in the composition by affine. Design: D3. Decided (pre-exec 2026-07-19): the editor keeps NO kind allowlist — enumerate ALL Registry-advertised kinds uniformly (arbitrariness is the core principle); kind-specific content acquisition (raster resolution, image/nested file/URI, solid color) is the kind's concern, surfaced generically, not a hard-coded editor set. Cell resolution is a first-class user input at insert (NOT a fixed default — resolves the D3/00-design.md:116 open marker): the user specifies resolution, then PLACES the cell in the overview wireframe to see how it fits the composition. That placement gesture wants editor.panels.overview (not yet built) — this leaf ships the model-level insert (resolution + affine) with a provisional placement; the overview-wireframe placement lands with/after editor.panels.overview. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.cells/model.md"
    }
    task selection "Project-level selection; hit-test; marquee; select-behind" {
      effort 2.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !model, editor.cameras.model
      note "One project-level selection (canvases are only cameras): click a cell BODY, a camera BORDER/label (interior click-through); marquee multi-select; Cmd/Ctrl-click select-behind. Shared across canvas/list/overview. Design: D7/D19. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.cells/selection.md"
    }
    task gizmo "Transform gizmo: move / scale / rotate / shear; pivot; snapping" {
      effort 3d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !selection
      note "Move (body), scale (corners proportional-by-default, edges 1D), rotate (Shift 15deg), shear (modifier), draggable pivot; snapping to cell edges/centers, camera frames, grid. Handle-drag is PLACEMENT, never resample. Design: D7/D8. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/gizmo.md"
    }
    task group_transform "Group transform: apply one affine delta across a multi-object selection" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !gizmo
      note "Transform a multi-object selection: apply one affine delta about a shared pivot (the union of the selected placed extents, interact::selected_extent, pick.hpp:136-137) across every selected cell's placing layer, coalesced into one journal entry via doc.transact(name).coalesce(key) and a batch commands::Command (the natural home for the transform_cells_command this leaf's single-object path did not need -- D-gizmo-3). Kept out of scope of editor.cells.gizmo because the single-object gizmo is the D7/SS6 primitive and the batch-coalesce is a distinct concern. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/gizmo.md. Design: docs/00-design.md D7/D8, docs/01-architecture.md A17. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/group_transform.md"
    }
    task resolution "Cell resolution display; resample-to-crisp; health badge" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !selection
      note "Show a cell's native/working resolution separately from placement; 'resample to crisp' grows a raster's grid; a referenced image is source-limited (the file is the floor). The health badge flags a camera out-resolving a cell. Design: D8. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/resolution.md"
    }
    task remove "Delete selected cell(s) via Document::remove_content + Delete chord" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !selection
      note "Delete the selected cell(s) via arbc::Document::remove_content(content, composition, layer) (document.hpp:131 -- atomic, one journal entry, undoable) with the Delete chord and a rail/inspector affordance plus an e2e. Insert without delete is the gap editor.cells.model opens; the library verb already exists, so this is mechanical. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor.cells/model.md. Design: docs/00-design.md D7/D15. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.cells/remove.md"
    }
    task insert_schema "Drive the insert dialog off the library's per-kind insert schema" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.canvas.arbc_v040, !model
      note "ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#21 shipped `arbc::KindInsertSchema` on the Registry in v0.4.0 — the fields a host must collect with their types, defaults, ranges and units, plus an `assemble` thunk turning collected strings into the kind's config, registered on the same atomic `Registry::add` as the factory (so a plugin cannot describe another kind's config after the fact) and read back through `Registry::insert_schema(id)`. The editor built its own `ace::scene::KindInsertSchema` + `InsertField` + per-kind grammar adapters in the meantime (src/scene/ace/scene/cell.hpp:37-99, src/scene/cell.cpp:207); that table is exactly what D-cells_model-2 predicted would shrink to zero once the API existed. Replace the editor's grammar adapters with the library's schema and its `assemble` thunk, so the grammar stays the KIND's own — the editor renders fields and hands back strings, and stops knowing that solid's separator is a comma. WHAT MUST NOT CHANGE: A16 / Constraint 2's no-allowlist property. `insert_schemas` still emits exactly one entry per `registry.ids()` entry, unconditionally and in registration order; a kind that registers NO schema upstream keeps the editor's raw-config fallback (`k_raw_config_field`, cell.hpp:66-72) and stays insertable end-to-end — that fallback is what makes the property testable and is NOT retired by this leaf. Also register a schema for the editor's OWN kind at src/scene/camera.cpp:441's `registry.add`, since the editor is now a plugin author by the same rule it applies to everyone else. RIDES ALONG — ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#22, org.arbc.solid's grammar now admits the extent the type always supported (`r,g,b,a,x,y,w,h` beside the unchanged `r,g,b,a`, origin plus size so a typo cannot express an inverted rect, non-positive size an error value): a Registry-built solid was always unbounded, so its layer transform was a NO-OP and placing, scaling or rotating one changed nothing on screen. That retires D-cells_model-3's accepted consequence and gives `scene::content_extent` a real answer for a factory-built solid instead of the honest `nullopt` (cell.hpp:100+), which is what `interact::place_in_view` needs BEFORE add_cell mints anything (Constraint 6). The built-ins default solid's extent upstream so an insert dialog produces a PLACEABLE solid. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md 'arbc Registry per-kind insert-schema hook' + 'org.arbc.solid factory grammar admits no bounds field'. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A16, docs/00-design.md D3, :505-511. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.cells/insert_schema.md"
    }
    task one_action_one_entry "One user-visible action is one journal entry for create and multi-delete" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.canvas.arbc_v040, !remove
      note "ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#20 shipped `Document::create_content_and_attach` (document.hpp:128) and `Document::remove_contents(std::span<const Removal>)` (:151) in v0.4.0; both publish once, append ONE entry, and reverse whole. Consume both. CREATE: `add_content` self-commits — it was the only vtable-binding call — so every cell and camera create costs two journal entries (content first, then transact -> add_layer -> attach_layer -> commit) and passes through a published state in which a content exists attached to nothing. src/scene/cell.cpp:305-308 says so in a comment and add_camera mirrors it (D-cells_model-7); both collapse to one call. DELETE: editor.cells.remove ships the correct single-object verb, but an N-object multi-select delete produces N entries and needs N undo presses to reverse something the user did ONCE (D-cells_remove-2); route it through `remove_contents` so a multi-select delete is one undo unit. The `(composition, layer)` naming and Constraint 5's leave-the-rest-untouched rule are unchanged — this changes journal GRANULARITY, not what is removed. PROOF OBLIGATION: assert entry COUNT, not just final state — the existing tests pass on both the two-entry and one-entry shapes, which is why the debt survived this long. Undo-once-restores-everything for an N-selection delete, and undo-once-removes-a-created-cell-whole, are the two cases that fail today. tests/cells_remove_test.cpp, tests/cells_remove_e2e_test.cpp and tests/cell_model_test.cpp carry them. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md 'arbc Document lacks atomic create-content-and-attach' + 'arbc batch removal verb'. Design: docs/00-design.md D15, docs/01-architecture.md A17. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.cells/one_action_one_entry.md"
    }
    task scoped_edit "Make insert/delete verbs honor the entered composition scope" {
      effort 2.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.panels.layers, !remove
      note "scene::remove_cells and add_cell currently hard-code root_composition; make them resolve the active composition (AppState::entered_composition) instead, so insert and delete operate on the entered nested scope when the user has entered one. The scope model and its entered_composition field ship with editor.panels.layers; this leaf owns the thin edit-plumbing that consumes it. src/scene/ace/scene/cell.hpp:157 comment already flags this gap. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor.panels/layers.md (Deferred WBS). Design: docs/00-design.md D17, docs/01-architecture.md A17. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.cells/scoped_edit.md"
    }
    task insert_offer "Drive the insert dialog off Registry::insert_offer, not ids()" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      depends editor.canvas.arbc_v070, editor.cells.insert_schema
      note "ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#37 shipped in v0.6.0: `KindMetadata::insertability` (a trailing field defaulting to `KindInsertability::Host`) plus the derived `Registry::insert_offer(id)`, answering `Schema`, `RawConfig` or `NotInsertable`. Upstream deliberately did NOT overload the existing lookup, because a NULL `insert_schema(id)` already means `no schema, but DO offer it` — the issue-#21 raw-config fallback, which stays first-class — and fusing those two cases would destroy the distinction a host most needs. `scene::insert_schemas` currently emits one entry per `registry.ids()` entry UNCONDITIONALLY (src/scene/cell.cpp, cell.hpp:36-42,85-88); switch it to ask `insert_offer` and drop `NotInsertable` kinds. THIS IS NOT A RETREAT FROM THE NO-ALLOWLIST RULE (A16) AND MUST NOT BECOME ONE: the editor still holds no kind list of its own and still asks the registry about every id — what changes is WHICH QUESTION it asks. A plugin registering an insertable kind still surfaces automatically; that property is the whole point and a test must pin it. TWO THINGS THIS FIXES AT ONCE. (1) `org.arbc.fade` and `org.arbc.crossfade` are registered kinds — they enter the registry through `register_builtin_kinds` via `FadeContent::kind_id`/`CrossfadeContent::kind_id` constants rather than string literals, which is why a casual grep for `org.arbc.` in builtin_kinds.cpp misses them and why the 2026-08-07 triage wrongly recorded only four registered kinds. There are SIX. Both have been listed in this editor's insert dialog all along, offering a raw-config box for factories that REFUSE EVERY CONFIG THERE IS (an operator's input edges cannot travel a ContentConfig), so the dialog has been showing two entries that can never succeed. v0.6.0 marks them Internal; this leaf is what makes the editor honour that. (2) `org.arbc.camera` — the editor's OWN kind, registered via `register_extra_kinds` so cameras round-trip — is offered too, and a camera minted from the dialog skips the identity/state setup `scene::add_camera` performs. Declare it `KindInsertability::Internal` at its registration site (src/commands/app_state.cpp:64, scene/camera.hpp:114). That closes the parked `org.arbc.camera insertability` item WITHOUT the D7/A14 allowlist amendment it looked like it needed — the fix is one field on the editor's own registration, which is exactly what filing #37 was for. THE SECOND AXIS, WHICH THIS LEAF ALSO CONSUMES (arbc#39, v0.7.0). An earlier draft of this note recorded `org.arbc.tone` as a limitation this leaf could NOT close: it registers Host-insertable (correctly — a user can genuinely mint one and an audio tool should offer it) and advertises a good schema, but `ToneContent::bounds()` returns an empty Rect and its render is a culled stub, so minting one HERE yields an invisible cell that can never show anything. v0.7.0 closes it: `KindMetadata::modality` declares what a kind PRODUCES, orthogonal to who may mint it, and `insert_offer(id, presentable)` filters both axes in ONE call so a host cannot satisfy one and forget the other. PASS `arbc::KindModality::Visual` — this editor is a still-image compositor and presents no audio. That excludes tone (Audio) while KEEPING nested and the operator kinds (Both, which intersects Visual), so the modality filter must NOT be written as an equality test; use the library's `intersects` semantics by passing `presentable` and trusting `insert_offer`, never by comparing modality values in the editor. Do NOT hard-code Visual as a scattered literal — name it once as the editor's presentable set beside the enumeration, so a future audio surface is a one-line change rather than a hunt. The no-allowlist property (A16) is preserved and must be pinned by a test: the editor still asks the registry about every id and still holds no kind list of its own; what changed is which question it asks. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md `org.arbc.camera insertability via the cell-insert dialog` (arbc#37). Design: docs/00-design.md D7; docs/01-architecture.md A16."
    }
    task resample_apply "Resample to crisp: grow a raster's working grid to the camera's density" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      depends editor.canvas.arbc_v070, editor.cells.resolution
      note "ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#31 shipped the generic `Resampleable` facet in v0.5.0, discovered via `Content::resampleable()` rather than allowlisted — exactly the shape the parked item asked for, so this leaf names NO concrete kind (A16). THE DISCOVERY HALF CARRIES AS MUCH AS THE VERB: a painted `org.arbc.raster` returns the facet; a referenced `org.arbc.image` keeps the `nullptr` default; so the host greys the action with an honest `source-limited` reason having named no kind — which is precisely what `editor.cells.resolution` already computes as `DetailSource` and shows in its health badge, so this leaf ADDS THE VERB to a surface that already states the problem. The interface (contract/resampleable.hpp): `Grid working_grid() const` — what the content holds now, in its OWN local pixel units, for comparing against the density the camera asks for; and `std::optional<Grid> resample(Model::Transaction&, ObjectId self, Grid target)` — transactional exactly like `RasterContent::paint`, returning the grid ACTUALLY ACHIEVED (a kind may CLAMP to what it can afford) or `nullopt` if it declined and published nothing. HANDLE THE CLAMP AND THE DECLINE AS FIRST-CLASS OUTCOMES, not as errors: a resample that lands at less than the requested grid still succeeded and the badge must re-read from `working_grid()` afterwards rather than from what was asked for. Wire it through a `commands::Command` so it is one journal entry, undoable, `ObjectId` preserved. Upstream also guarantees the resampled pixels are BYTE-IDENTICAL to what a render at that density produces, so there is one sampling policy rather than two — which makes a golden that renders the cell before and after at the target density the natural proof. The `bounds()`-read/write TSan case tasks/refinements/editor/selection.md Open question 2 anticipated belongs here: `RasterContent::bounds()` is now derived from the live tile table (v0.5.0), so a resample MOVES a cell's extent under a concurrent UI-thread `pick_targets` read. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md `libarbc has no content-resample verb` (arbc#31); tasks/refinements/editor/resolution.md D-resolution-5. Design: docs/00-design.md D8; docs/01-architecture.md A16."
    }
    task objectid_field_picker "Render ObjectId insert-schema field as a composition picker, not free text" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.canvas.arbc_v070, editor.cells.insert_schema
      note "The v0.5.0 org.arbc.nested insert schema advertises one `child` field of type `ObjectId` (min 1, no default). editor.canvas.arbc_v070 added the `InsertFieldType::ObjectId` enumerator so the hint survives map_field_type, and the L3 insert modal currently renders it as a labelled text field — adequate for the pin leaf, not adequate for the user. This leaf upgrades the rendering: an `InsertFieldType::ObjectId` field is rendered as a picker over existing compositions (all contents in the document with a composition, i.e. org.arbc.nested cells), the chosen ObjectId is resolved back into the field's collected string. Holds no kind allowlist (A16 preserved — the picker applies to any field the registry declares ObjectId, not only org.arbc.nested). Wire an ImGui Test Engine e2e confirming the picker appears and a selected composition resolves. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/canvas/arbc_v070.md. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A16; docs/00-design.md D3. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.cells/objectid_field_picker.md"
    }
  }

  # ---- Info panels (D5/D6/D10/D11) ----
  task panels "Info panels" {
    task inspector "Dense property sheet (transform / appearance / z-order / source)" {
      effort 2.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.cells.selection, editor.dock.view_registry
      note "A dense property sheet for the project selection: transform (units), appearance & stacking (opacity/blend/z-order), source (referenced/owned info + health). Design: D7 (density) / D11. Refinement: tasks/refinements/panels/inspector.md"
    }
    task layers "Layers list (+ cameras); reorder; referenced-vs-painted; nested expand / enter" {
      effort 3d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !inspector, editor.cells.model
      note "The layer list (cameras section + layers front->back); reorder = z-order; the borrowed/owned (referenced-vs-painted) distinction shown; a nested cell can be expanded (peek) or entered (isolation scope + breadcrumb). Design: D6/D11/D17. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.panels/layers.md"
    }
    task overview "Patterned-fill wireframe overview; z-order occlusion; editable" {
      effort 4d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !layers, editor.cameras.model
      note "The render-free overview: each cell a hatch pattern (shared identity with the list), front fills occlude back with always-on-top dotted borders (z-order without a list), cameras as frames, the live viewport highlighted; editable (drag to place) + navigator + export/shot map. Design: D6/§5. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/overview.md. Decided (pre-exec 2026-07-19): hatch density is CONTENT-space (scales/tilts with the cell so scale/orientation read for free). The overview is a full viewport NAVIGATOR — manipulate the active viewport with an in-overview zoom control (partners D2 deep-zoom aids, editor.canvas.nav_aids), NOT navigation-only — and it is the placement surface for editor.cells.model inserts (a new cell is placed here). Remaining open items are visual polish only (hatch style/opacity, pattern-count-before-color, camera visual language)."
    }
    task hatch_swatch "List-side hatch swatch + cross-highlight between list and overview" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !overview
      note "Draw the deterministic interact::overview_pattern swatch beside each Layers-list row and wire AppState hover cross-highlight between the list and the overview (§5:191-194: 'the same pattern swatch appears beside the layer row … hovering either cross-highlights the other'). The overview side of the identity ships with editor.panels.overview; this leaf owns the list-side rendering and the cross-panel hover state. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/overview.md. Design: docs/00-design.md D6/§5. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.panels/hatch_swatch.md"
    }
    task overview_gizmo "Full scale/rotate/shear gizmo on schematic overview boxes" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !overview, editor.cells.gizmo
      note "Full 8-handle scale/rotate/shear transform gizmo on schematic boxes in the overview, reusing the shipped interact cell-gizmo handle math (editor.cells.gizmo) fed through the overview transform. §6 makes the overview the coarse-arrangement surface (move + z-order shipped in editor.panels.overview); the full gizmo-in-overview is real, separable draw+dispatch work. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/overview.md. Design: docs/00-design.md D7/D8/§6. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.panels/overview_gizmo.md"
    }
    task color "sRGB picker; eyedropper (through camera); linear boundary" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.dock.view_registry, editor.paint.brush, editor.canvas.tool_dispatch
      note "A conventional sRGB/HSV/hex picker + straight-alpha opacity; the editor is the invisible translator to/from the library's premultiplied-linear working space. Eyedropper samples the displayed sRGB through the active camera. Design: D10. Refinement: tasks/refinements/panels/color.md"
    }
    task color_eyedrop_cell "Eyedropper modifier: active-cell own straight color via isolated render" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.panels.color
      note "D10 eyedropper modifier that samples the active cell's own straight color (not the composite): render only the selected cell in isolation through the active camera at the sample point, unpremultiply → linear_to_srgb8 → set_active_color, bound to the eyedropper modifier (e.g. Alt-click). Separable from the base composited eyedropper (editor.panels.color) because it needs isolated single-cell rendering (siblings hidden). Design: D10 (docs/00-design.md:477). Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/panels/color.md. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.panels/color_eyedrop_cell.md"
    }
    task color_eyedrop_nested "Eyedropper modifier: nested/operator cells via ephemeral single-object composition" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.panels.color_eyedrop_cell
      note "Extend the active-cell eyedropper modifier to nested compositions and operator/procedural cells, which sample_cell_color currently returns std::nullopt for because bare render_layer cannot stand up the pull service they need. Render the one cell in isolation through an ephemeral single-object composition (rebuild the content via the registry factory into an anonymous Document, anchor a Viewport at it, and arbc::render_offline through the full pull-service pipeline — src/runtime/offline.cpp:44-67), then decode as the shipped leaf does; the modifier stops falling back to the composite for those cells. NOTE: the refinement REVERSED the ephemeral-composition approach this title and note describe — D-eyedrop_nested-1 anchors render_offline at the cell's composition_ref() on the REAL PINNED document instead, because an ephemeral rebuild cannot reproduce a nested-within-nested child's own pull closure. Read the refinement, not this note, for what shipped. Design: D10 (docs/00-design.md:477). Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor.panels/color_eyedrop_cell.md. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.panels/color_eyedrop_nested.md"
    }
    task history "History view/panel: journal entry list + click-to-jump" {
      effort 2.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.project.undo, editor.dock.view_registry
      note "Render journal().entry_at(i).name as an ordered list with the cursor() position marked; 'Undo <name>'/'Redo <name>' labels; click-an-entry to jump (loop undo()/redo() to reach the target cursor — single-step navigation only). L3 views. Design: D15/D18/D21. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/undo.md. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/history.md"
    }
  }

  # ---- Painting (D4/D5/D12) ----
  task paint "Painting" {
    task brush "Brush tool; %-of-view size + ring; dab into org.arbc.raster" {
      effort 3d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.cells.model, editor.canvas.tool_dispatch
      note "The brush tool: size in % of view (shorter edge, log slider) with an on-canvas ring; strokes dab into an org.arbc.raster cell's editable facet, coalesced into one undo step. Design: D5. Refinement: tasks/refinements/paint/brush.md. Decided (pre-exec 2026-07-19): brush size is SCREEN-locked only — a canvas-locked (composition-units) mode is deliberately NOT offered; it contradicts the model's principle (brush is screen-relative % of view; resolution is cell-owned, detail floor at the cell). No size-lock toggle."
    }
    task paint_res "Cell-fixed resolution painting; screen->cell mapping; detail-floor readout" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !brush
      note "Dabs rasterize into the CELL's fixed working grid; the brush footprint maps camera px -> composition units -> cell px by affine composition. The '~N px on <cell>' readout doubles as the resolution-health / detail-floor cue. Design: D4/D5. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/paint_res.md"
    }
    task retouch_stack "Add-paint-layer-above a referenced photo (non-destructive)" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      depends !brush, editor.import.image
      note "Attempting to paint a read-only (referenced) photo offers a one-gesture 'add a retouch layer above' — a new owned raster over its bounds; the original is never edited in place. Design: D12. Refinement: tasks/refinements/retouch_stack.md"
    }
  }

  # ---- Import & assets (D11/D12/D13) ----
  task import "Import & assets" {
    task image "Drop / place an image -> borrowed cell (1:1 px->units)" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.cells.model
      note "Drop or Place an image file -> a borrowed (referenced) read-only org.arbc.image cell at the drop point, sized native px -> composition units 1:1 so pixel dimensions carry real relative scale. Design: D11/D12. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/image.md"
    }
    task paste "Paste / clipboard bitmap -> owned image" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !image
      note "A pasted bitmap has no source file to borrow, so the project MINTS it as an owned read-only image in assets/; otherwise it behaves like an import. Design: D11/D12. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/paste.md"
    }
    task owned_asset_republish "Copy owned image blobs into a fresh root on Save As / Consolidate" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      depends editor.import.paste
      note "On Save As / Consolidate into a fresh root, copy each document-referenced owned image blob from the source assets/ into the target root's assets/ so a pasted image survives a copy to a new root (scratch->Save-As persistence). In-place Save->reopen round-trips without it; only a different-root copy needs the republish step. The image codec writes only params.source (never routing bytes through the sink), so project::save_project against a different root leaves the target assets/ missing the blob. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/paste.md (D-paste-5, Constraint 6). Design: D11/D12/D13, docs/01-architecture.md A30."
    }
    task nested "Place another .arbc -> nested composition cell" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !image
      note "Place another project (.arbc) -> an org.arbc.nested cell referencing it (the library's external nested reference). Design: D12. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/nested.md"
    }
    task nested_live_resolve "A freshly-placed nested reference resolves in the placing session" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      depends editor.canvas.arbc_v070, editor.import.nested
      note "ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#32 shipped `install_external_composition(doc, bridge, registry, reference, base_uri, assets = nullptr, tiles = nullptr)` in v0.5.0 (document_serialize.hpp:378-381) — the seam that installs an external composition into an ALREADY-OPEN document. `editor.import.nested` ships the honest v1: a freshly placed `org.arbc.nested` cell renders the doc-05 placeholder in the placing session and resolves on the next reopen, because `ExternalCompositionLoader` was LoadContext-scoped and unreachable after load. Call the new seam from the WRITER THREAD right after the nested cell is placed, feeding the editor's existing `FilesystemAssetSource`; `assets` defaults to the source the document's own load recorded, so passing nothing is usually right. THREE OUTCOMES, ALL VALUES: resolved / pending / unavailable — the last is NOT an error, and the placeholder path this leaf replaces stays the correct rendering for it, so do not turn an unavailable child into a failed placement. DEDUP IS THE LIBRARY'S: installing a URI the document already holds returns that composition and parses nothing, document-wide and durable, so placing the same .arbc twice must NOT be special-cased here. THE ORDERING SUBTLETY THAT MUST NOT BE GOT WRONG: the install is DELIBERATELY NOT UNDOABLE. The child arriving is not the user's edit — the PLACEMENT is, and that is what the host commits in its own transaction. So an undo of the placement leaves the child installed and ready, and a redo re-binds it with NO second parse and no re-read of a file that may have moved since. Do not fold the install into the placement transaction to make it `tidy`; that is the design upstream explicitly rejected, and it would make a redo depend on the filesystem. The shipped save->reopen round-trip golden (import_nested_64x64.rgba8) must pass UNMODIFIED — this leaf changes when the child resolves, never what it composites to. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md `Live in-session resolution of a freshly-placed org.arbc.nested external reference` (arbc#32); tasks/refinements/editor/nested.md D-nested-3. Design: docs/00-design.md D11; docs/01-architecture.md A4.1."
    }
    task consolidate "Consolidate project (borrowed -> owned + relativize URIs) + relink missing borrows" {
      effort 2.5d
      allocate team
      depends editor.import.paste, editor.import.nested, editor.project.undo_reconcile
      note "Consolidate copies borrowed files into assets/ and rewrites their URIs to project-relative (a reversible scene transaction, D15), producing a self-contained portable bundle; relink surfaces a locate-file / reveal affordance for a moved borrow. Design: D11/D12/D13, docs/00-design.md §8 (Import & assets: Consolidate, missing/moved -> placeholder + relink), D15 (consolidate reversible -> a dispatched transaction). Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/gc.md RE-SCOPED AT THE v0.5.0 TRIAGE (2026-08-08): the refinement `tasks/refinements/editor/import.consolidate.md` was written against v0.4.1, where `Document` could mint a content and remove one and NOTHING IN BETWEEN — so D-consolidate-2 chose a batched delete+re-insert, accepting a NEW ObjectId per consolidated cell as the price. ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#34 shipped `Document::update_content_config(id, params, name)` in v0.5.0: an in-place, versioned, journalled rewrite that PRESERVES the ObjectId, one call per cell being one journal entry and one undo press with the record slot, the layer binding and every reference intact. USE IT — and re-open D-consolidate-2 rather than implementing the superseded decision, because the id churn it accepted is exactly what made selection, per-cell UI state and any org.arbc.nested reference from another composition silently miss after a Consolidate, and the host cannot reach inside a nested reference to rewrite it. TWO PREREQUISITES THE REFINEMENT DOES NOT KNOW ABOUT. (1) `update_content_config` REFUSES unless a `ContentReconstruct` hook is installed via `Document::set_content_reconstruct` — a document with no hook keeps exactly its pre-#34 behaviour. `codec_content_reconstruct(codecs, registry, ctx)` is the ready-made hook, but it borrows a `LoadContext` that MUST OUTLIVE THE DOCUMENT, and this editor treats its LoadContext as transient to one load (D-open-7, src/project/project_open.cpp:97). Reconciling that lifetime is real design work this leaf owns — it is the one genuinely non-mechanical part, and discovering it at implementation time would be expensive. (2) The undo side is `editor.project.undo_reconcile`, now a dependency: history restores the params on its own, but the id->Content side-map is runtime state beside the model and must be reconciled, so an undo through the raw `journal()` would restore the params while leaving the live object built from the old ones. The kind is not changeable and every failure is a value that publishes nothing, so a refused rewrite must leave the batch atomic. The other parking-lot item this leaf named (the GC gap) closed independently in the same release and is now editor.project.gc_owned_images."
    }
  }

  # ---- Packaging & CI (native ship) ----
  task packaging "Packaging & CI" {
    task ci "Editor CI lanes (Linux / macOS / Windows)" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      depends editor.foundation.build
      note "Per-platform CI building the editor and running a headless smoke (window init + one rendered frame), matching the library's compiler matrix. Refinement: tasks/refinements/ci.md. Decided (pre-exec 2026-07-19): v1 is LINUX-ONLY — the full build + headless offscreen-GL smoke reusing the existing llvmpipe/act lane (docs/01-architecture.md:210-245). macOS and Windows lanes (their offscreen-GL rendered-frame smoke needs ANGLE/other, no llvmpipe) are DEFERRED to editor.packaging.package, which owns the release matrix. Title says tri-platform but scope here is Linux; retitle/rescope when packaging picks up mac/Win."
    }
    task package "Installable editor artifacts per platform" {
      effort 3d
      allocate team
      depends editor.panels.overview, editor.panels.color, editor.cameras.export, editor.paint.paint_res, editor.paint.retouch_stack, editor.import.paste, editor.import.nested, editor.cells.gizmo, editor.cells.resolution, editor.canvas.nav, editor.canvas.tool_dispatch, editor.cameras.manip, editor.cameras.look_through, editor.project.exec_new, editor.project.undo, editor.project.gc, editor.dock.tool_rail, editor.dock.workspaces
      note "Bundle the editor as an installable artifact per platform (AppImage/.dmg/.msi or CPack equivalents), embedding fonts/icons and staging the runtime. Gathers the feature-complete editor. Refinement: tasks/refinements/package.md"
    }
  }

  # ---- Post-v1 (deliberately outside M9E's gating set) ----
  #
  # Work decided as WANTED but OUT OF v1 SCOPE. A task lands here instead of in
  # tasks/parking-lot.md once the judgment call has been made and the answer is
  # "yes, but not now" — the parking lot is for undecided items, and an item with
  # a decision recorded against it does not belong there. M9E does NOT depend on
  # this group, so nothing here can gate v1 or surface as v1-ready work.
  task postv1 "Post-v1" {
    task blend_mode "Per-layer blend mode in the Inspector appearance block" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      depends editor.canvas.arbc_v070, editor.panels.inspector
      note "DECIDED at the 2026-08-09 triage: wanted, but NOT in v1. libarbc v0.6.0 (arbc#36) put a `BlendMode` on `LayerRecord`, set through `Model::Transaction::set_blend` and read through `LayerRecord::blend()`, over the SEPARABLE modes of PDF 1.4 / CSS Compositing Level 1 (multiply, screen, overlay, darken, lighten, colour-dodge, colour-burn, hard-light, soft-light, difference, exclusion). Add a mode dropdown to the Inspector's appearance block beside the opacity slider and visibility toggle (src/app/inspector_panel.cpp:70-88), following the shipped one-transaction mould EXACTLY: `scene::set_cell_opacity` resolves the placing layer and does `txn.set_opacity(layer, value)` on the EXISTING record, so the cell keeps its ObjectId, placement and order (src/scene/cell.cpp:596-617). A `scene::set_cell_blend` beside it doing `txn.set_blend(...)` is the whole L1 change. WHY IT WAS DEFERRED, so the reasoning is not relitigated from scratch: not a colour-correctness objection — that argument was considered and RETRACTED. arbc evaluates blend in the composition's working space, which here is arbc's default `k_working_rgba32f`, premultiplied linear-light sRGB (the editor never calls `set_working_space`, only reads it). So a Multiply here differs from a gamma-space Multiply in Photoshop/GIMP/CSS. That sounds disqualifying and is not: multiply is a PRODUCT, and under a pure power law a product commutes with encoding, so its divergence is tiny (roughly 3 levels in 255 at mid-grey), while OPACITY is a linear interpolation and diverges enormously (roughly 60 levels in 255 for 50% white over black) — and opacity already ships. The modes that genuinely diverge are the ones with a 0.5 threshold or mixed operations (overlay, soft-light, hard-light, colour-dodge/burn), because 0.5 is a different luminance in each space; worth a note in the UI if that ever bites. The ACTUAL reason for deferral is v1 scope discipline: no design row demands blend, the v1 modal set is closed at {Select, Brush, Eyedropper, Pan}, and blend belongs to a richer layer workflow v1 does not otherwise have — the same call D-color-5 made for the fg/bg colour pair. D10 needs NO amendment: it constrains the SPACE (`compositing stays linear`, no v1 blend-space toggle), not the mode vocabulary, and this leaf adds no space toggle. NOTHING IS LOST BY WAITING, verified rather than assumed: a foreign document's blend mode already round-trips through this editor with ZERO editor code, because `project::save` delegates wholesale to `arbc::serialize_snapshot` (src/project/save.cpp:178) and arbc preserves even an UNRECOGNISED blend name verbatim and re-emits it unchanged (reader.cpp:267,295-299). THE ONE PATTERN THAT WOULD BREAK THAT is editing by delete-and-re-insert, which drops the whole LayerRecord including its blend; no shipped edit verb does it, and editor.import.consolidate was re-scoped off it onto `update_content_config` for an unrelated reason — keep it that way. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md `Per-layer blend mode` (decided and removed 2026-08-09). Design: docs/00-design.md D10 (unchanged); docs/01-architecture.md A16."
    }
  }
}
